# Fuller rules for the front of the book: (title, text)
RULES = {
    "sudoku": "Every row, every column and every 3 by 3 box must contain the digits 1 to 9 once each. Start with the digit that appears most often and ask where it can still go in each box.",
    "killer": "The usual sudoku rules apply, but no numbers are given. Instead the grid is divided into dotted cages. The digits in a cage add up to the small number in its corner, and a digit never repeats inside a cage. Look for cages with only one possible set of digits: a two-cell cage totalling 3 must be 1 and 2; one totalling 17 must be 8 and 9. Remember that every row, column and box adds up to 45.",
    "kakuro": "A crossword made of numbers. Each run of white cells, across or down, adds up to the total in the dark cell where the run begins: totals above the diagonal belong to the run going across, totals below it to the run going down. Use only 1 to 9, and never repeat a digit within a run. Start with totals that can be made only one way, such as 3 in two cells (1 and 2) or 23 in three (6, 8 and 9).",
    "futoshiki": "Fill the grid so that each row and each column holds every number once. Where two cells have a sign between them, the open end faces the larger number and the point faces the smaller. Chains of signs are the way in: three cells joined by signs in a grid of five leave very few choices.",
    "calcudoku": "Each row and column holds every number once. The heavy lines mark cages. The clue in a cage gives a target and a sum: the numbers in the cage must make that target by adding, multiplying, subtracting or dividing. For subtraction and division, which only appear in two-cell cages, the numbers may be in either order. A number may repeat within a cage as long as it is not in the same row or column.",
    "skyscrapers": "Each cell holds a tower. Each row and column contains every height once. A number outside the grid tells you how many towers are visible looking along that row or column from that side: a taller tower hides every shorter one behind it. A clue equal to the grid size means the towers climb in order; a clue of 1 means the tallest tower stands at that edge.",
    "binary": "Fill every cell with a 0 or a 1. Each row and each column has equal numbers of each. No more than two of the same digit may sit next to each other in a line. Finally, no two rows are identical and no two columns are identical. Begin by blocking any pair: two 1s side by side must have a 0 at each end.",
    "nonogram": "The numbers beside each row and above each column tell you how long the shaded blocks are in that line, in order, with at least one empty cell between blocks. For example, 3 1 means a block of three, a gap, then a single cell. Shade what must be shaded and mark with a dot what must be empty. When you finish, the shaded cells make a picture; the solution tells you what it is.",
    "bridges": "Each circle is an island, and its number is how many bridges connect to it. Bridges run in straight lines across or down between islands. They may not cross each other or pass over an island, and no more than two bridges may join the same pair of islands. When you have finished, it must be possible to travel from any island to any other.",
    "wordsearch": "The listed words are hidden in the grid in straight lines. Ignore spaces and apostrophes in the list: PENNINE WAY appears as PENNINEWAY.",
    "wordsearch_hard": "These give you a theme and a number, and no list. Words may run in any of the eight directions, including backwards and diagonally upwards. Names of more than one word are written without spaces. The words are listed with the solution.",
    "cryptogram": "A sentence has been put into a simple code: each letter of the alphabet has been swapped for another, and the swap is the same all the way through. Look for one-letter words, which are almost always A or I, for repeated short words, and for the pattern after an apostrophe. Where letters are given, they are filled in for you.",
    "ladder": "Turn the top word into the bottom word by changing one letter at each step and leaving the others where they are. Every rung must be a real word. There is exactly the right number of rungs for the shortest route; other routes of the same length may exist.",
    "logic": "A small mystery with a single answer. Read each clue and note what it rules in and what it rules out, then combine clues. Nobody shares an activity, a place, an age or a month. Use the table to record your answer, and a scrap of paper for working.",
    "anagrams": "Each line is a single word with its letters shuffled. All ten answers belong to the theme at the top of the page.",
}


